EldenNet with GraceUI, the spikes S0-E and S4 (package @LABEL@)
@UNDERLINE@

This package runs two in-game spikes on your own PC, with GraceUI, EldenNet's menu and overlay. S0-E checks that
GraceUI's overlay works beside EldenNet and beside another overlay, in either order and whether it is loaded early or
late. S4 checks that nothing you type reaches the game while one of GraceUI's panels holds the controls. In each run a
small observer of GraceUI's writes a report of numbers, which you send back to us. EldenNet makes no call into the
game in any of these runs: it starts, shows its banner and goes no further. Nothing in this package is for playing with
yet.

Built from EldenNet at commit @COMMIT@ and from GraceUI @GRACE_VERSION@ at commit @GRACE_COMMIT@ (its test build),
with clang-cl (Release). Never shared: a test build for you alone, labelled @LABEL@.


How long it takes
-----------------

About 40 minutes for all six runs: about 4 minutes each for A1, A2, A3, A4 and A0, about 10 for B, and a few to set
up and to send the files. Each run stands alone: you can stop after any of them and send what you have.


What is in it
-------------

  eldennet-launcher.exe   EldenNet's launcher: backs up your saves, starts the game offline without Easy Anti-Cheat,
                          loads the run's DLLs, and watches the game until it ends.
  eldennet.dll            EldenNet in the game: the release build. In these runs it stops before its one call into
                          the game and shows its banner instead.
  gal\                    What R0c found, which EldenNet checks your game against as it starts, as in R1's package:
                          numbers and names of the design's own, nothing taken from the game.
  GraceUI\                GraceUI @GRACE_VERSION@, its test build (the same code as a release, with read-only counters
                          for the observer): graceui.dll, its version file graceui.toml, its fonts, its licence and its
                          notices.
  grace-spike.dll         GraceUI's observer: it shows the notice "GraceUI spike: recording. F9 opens the typing
                          test." and writes the run's report, graceui-spike.jsonl.
  grace-standin.dll       A stand-in for another overlay, of GraceUI's own, which counts the game's frames on their way
                          to the screen. It is never part of a release.
  run-A1.cmd, run-A2.cmd, run-A3.cmd, run-A4.cmd, run-A0.cmd, run-B.cmd
                          One file for each run: it starts eldennet-launcher.exe from this folder with that run's
                          options, and names nothing outside this folder (open one in Notepad to read it).
  README-SPIKES.txt       This file.
  SHA256SUMS              The SHA-256 of every other file in this package.
  LICENSE.txt             EldenNet's licence (MIT).
  THIRD_PARTY.md          What EldenNet is built with, and its credits.
  THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt The licences of the libraries inside EldenNet's programs. GraceUI's files carry their own:
                          GraceUI\LICENSE.txt and GraceUI\NOTICE.txt.


Before you start
----------------

  - Close the game, and start Steam.
  - Unzip this package into a folder of its own, anywhere but the game's folder.
  - Copy the GraceUI folder from this package into the game's own Game folder, beside eldenring.exe (in Steam:
    right-click ELDEN RING, then Manage, then Browse local files, and open Game). Runs A3, A4 and B need it there: in
    them EldenNet loads GraceUI itself, from the game's folder, where a GraceUI release will be installed. The game
    never loads it by itself, and you can delete it again once you have finished.
  - Turn on EldenNet's F11 view, which is off unless its settings say otherwise; runs A1 to A4 use it. In PowerShell
    (Windows Terminal's default), run these two lines:

      New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force "$env:APPDATA\EldenNet\config"
      Set-Content -Encoding ascii "$env:APPDATA\EldenNet\config\settings.toml" "schema = 2", "debug = { panel = true }"

    If %APPDATA%\EldenNet\config\settings.toml exists already, run only the first line, open the file in Notepad, and
    put panel = true into its debug line instead (for example debug = { panel = true }), keeping what else it holds.
  - The launcher backs up your saves before it starts the game (in %APPDATA%\EldenNet\save-backups). If you want a
    copy of your own as well, copy the folder %APPDATA%\EldenRing somewhere first.
  - The game will run offline, without Easy Anti-Cheat. Use a save you are happy to play offline, and never take a save
    played with mods into online play.
  - Each run's file opens a console window, where the launcher's messages appear, behind the game. Setting the game to
    windowed mode first makes them easy to read; otherwise press Alt+Tab to look, and Alt+Tab back.
  - You will press F5 (GraceUI's menu), F11 (EldenNet's view), F9 (the typing test, in run B) and, if the Steam overlay
    shows in this game, Shift+Tab.
  - Windows or your security software may warn about an unsigned program. Allow it only if the SHA-256 values match
    (below); otherwise stop and tell us.


The steps
---------

Run them in this order: A1, A2, A3, A4, A0 and B. For each run, double-click its file in this folder (or open a command
prompt here and type its name, run-A1.cmd for the first; in PowerShell, put .\ in front: .\run-A1.cmd). The console
window opens and the game starts. The first time, the launcher asks where eldenring.exe is if it cannot find it through
Steam, and shows its notices. Do the run's steps in the game, then quit from the game's own menu. Once the game has
ended, the console says "Run A1 has ended" (or B, and so on): press a key to close it, and go on to the next run, or
stop there.

Runs A1 to A4: S0-E with EldenNet (about 4 minutes each)

  Run   File         GraceUI loaded                          Injected
  A1    run-A1.cmd   by the launcher, before EldenNet        early, as the game starts
  A2    run-A2.cmd   by the launcher, before EldenNet        late, once the game's window shows
  A3    run-A3.cmd   by EldenNet itself, after the window    early, as the game starts
  A4    run-A4.cmd   by EldenNet itself, after the window    late, once the game's window shows

In the game:

  1. At the title screen, check that GraceUI's notice "GraceUI spike: recording" and EldenNet's banner ("Spike run:
     EldenNet makes no call into the game") both show.
  2. Load a save.
  3. Press F5: GraceUI's menu opens. Look round it for a few seconds, then press F5 again to close it.
  4. Press F11: EldenNet's view opens. Press F11 again to close it.
  5. If the Steam overlay shows in this game (Shift+Tab), open it and close it again, and note whether GraceUI's notice
     is still drawn afterwards.
  6. Play for two minutes, then quit from the game's own menu.

Run A0: S0-E with GraceUI alone (about 4 minutes; run-A0.cmd)

GraceUI, the observer and the stand-in, without EldenNet, injected early. The same steps as A1's, without F11: at the
title screen only GraceUI's notice shows, as EldenNet is not loaded. This tells a fault of GraceUI's own from one that
comes only with EldenNet beside it.

Run B: S4, input (about 10 minutes; run-B.cmd)

With EldenNet, injected early; EldenNet loads GraceUI.

  1. Load a save and stand somewhere safe, away from enemies.
  2. Press F9: the typing test opens in the middle of the screen and takes the keyboard, the mouse and the pad.
  3. For 60 seconds, type anything (W, A, S and D among it, and some held down) and move the mouse about. What you
     type shows in the test's own field, on screen only: the report counts it and never keeps it. The test closes by
     itself after 60 seconds; holding Esc for a second stops it early.
  4. Note whether the Tarnished moved or the camera turned at any time while the test was open.
  5. Hold W so the Tarnished walks, press F5 while still holding it, let W go, then press F5 again: the Tarnished must
     stand still once the menu is open, and must not start walking again when it closes.
  6. If you have a pad: open GraceUI's menu with F5, then press B twice (the first goes back to the list on the left,
     the second closes the menu): the Tarnished must not dodge or roll.
  7. Once: in GraceUI's menu, on the Menu page, set Interface scale to 1.50, close the menu and open it again. Then set
     it back.
  8. Quit from the game's own menu.

If you have them, the Steam floating keyboard and Proton are rows of their own: tell us what happened.


What to send back
-----------------

  %APPDATA%\EldenNet\spikes\                   the whole folder: required
  %APPDATA%\EldenNet\logs\eldennet.log         helpful
  %APPDATA%\EldenNet\logs\launcher.log         helpful
  %APPDATA%\EldenNet\crashes\                  the newest file there, only if the game crashed

(Type %APPDATA%\EldenNet into Explorer's address bar to open the folder.) The spikes folder holds one folder for each
run, each with its graceui-spike.jsonl: s0e-1 to s0e-4 for A1 to A4, s0e_alone-1 for A0 and s4-1 for B when you ran
them once each in order (each run takes the next free number), and the launcher's own short log of each. Tell us if you
ran one twice or in another order.

With them, your answers to:

  - Did the Tarnished stay still and the camera stay put during the typing test (run B)? (yes or no)
  - If the Steam overlay showed: was GraceUI still drawing after it closed? (yes or no)


What it does
------------

  - Backs up your saves (it only copies them), checks that Steam is running and that the game is not, and checks that
    your game is the build R0c read (2.7.2.0, worldwide). If the launcher says the build is not supported, stop and
    tell us: the game has been updated since R0c.
  - Starts the game offline without Easy Anti-Cheat, by writing the game's Steam app id into steam_appid.txt beside
    the game while it starts, and removing it again (eldennet-launcher.exe --cleanup removes it if a crash left it).
  - Before the game starts, makes the run's folder in %APPDATA%\EldenNet\spikes and tells the observer its name.
  - Loads the run's DLLs into the game, in the order its file gives: GraceUI, its observer and the stand-in, and
    EldenNet (not in run A0). In runs A3, A4 and B, EldenNet loads GraceUI from the game's folder itself.
  - EldenNet reads the game's code and data as they are loaded, read-only and through guarded copies, to check that
    your game is the build it knows, then stops before its one call into the game and shows its banner, "Spike run:
    EldenNet makes no call into the game". It makes no call into the game for the rest of the run.
  - GraceUI puts itself between the game and Windows' own functions for drawing a frame and for reading the keyboard,
    the mouse and the pad, draws its notices, menu and panels over the game, and keeps input from the game while one
    of its panels holds it.
  - Every 5 seconds the observer writes a line of its report into the run's folder: numbers, fixed words and our own
    labels only; no path, no name, nothing of your character or your PC, and of what you type only how many
    characters.
  - Watches the game until it ends, and keeps a short record if it ends badly: the error's code, which module it was
    in and the time, never a memory dump.


What it never does
------------------

  - It never goes online, and the game cannot reach FromSoftware's servers without Easy Anti-Cheat.
  - It never changes your saves or the game's files (steam_appid.txt aside, which it removes; the GraceUI folder in
    the game's folder is the one you copied there).
  - It never calls into the game in these runs: EldenNet's banner says so. GraceUI, its observer and the stand-in
    never read or write the game's memory and never call the game's code.
  - It never keeps what you type: the typing test shows it on screen only, and the report counts it.
  - It never runs anything else, reaches the internet, keeps keys or sends anything anywhere. You send the files
    yourself.


If the game crashes or freezes
------------------------------

  - Your saves were backed up before the game started: each backup is a folder in %APPDATA%\EldenNet\save-backups,
    named by date and time, with a manifest of the files' sizes and hashes. The game's own save is not touched by
    EldenNet; only if the game itself reports a damaged save, copy ER0000.sl2 (and ER0000.sl2.bak) from the newest
    backup back into %APPDATA%\EldenRing\<the same number>\, with the game closed.
  - If the game freezes, close it with Alt+F4, or end eldenring.exe in Task Manager.
  - Send the files above, the newest crash record included, and say which run it was. The next start of the launcher
    explains it once. You can go on with the next run.
  - To play normally again, start the game from Steam as usual. Nothing of EldenNet or GraceUI stays loaded, and the
    GraceUI folder in the game's folder does nothing unless EldenNet loads it.


Check the files
---------------

In PowerShell, in this folder:

  Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 eldennet-launcher.exe, eldennet.dll, grace-spike.dll, grace-standin.dll, GraceUI\graceui.dll

Each value must equal its line in SHA256SUMS (letter case aside).
